  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled weather expected through midweek as a \nfront meanders around the area. Turning noticeably cooler by \nWednesday.\n\nLooking at frontal boundary across our area today, with several\nvorts moving along it aloft and should see a decent shot of\nshowers/storms by late this afternoon and especially tonight\ninto Tuesday. The front drops south Tuesday into Wednesday \nwhile we get wedged in.\n\nToday will be the last warm day, and depending on how much\nsunshine we get, could see some stronger storms develop, but\nagain looks isolated. As far as heavy rain/localized flooding,\nthere will be a potential for that, with the 00z HREF\nprobabilities for more than 3\" rain in 12 hrs highlighting the\nPiedmont of NC tonight. Still models/ensembles are blotchy with\nthis coverage and not seeing a need for any flood watches.\n\nAs we head into Tuesday, will have to watch where the heavy rain\noccurs today-tonight, and whether we may need flood watches. \n\nThe bigger story will be the significant cool down with \npotential to remain stuck in the 50s and 60s Wednesday and \nThursday. Cold air advection is expected to persist for multiple\ndays so it may take until the weekend to return to readings near\nnormal.",
